{
  "raw": {
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Visiting-Clinical-Instructor-of-Education%2C-Literacy/44327-en_US": {
      "title": "Visiting Clinical Instructor of Education, Literacy",
      "description": "Visiting Clinical Instructor of Education, Literacy Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44327 Job Title: Visiting Clinical Instructor of Education, Literacy City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary The College of Humanities, Education and Social Sciences at Purdue University Northwest announces an exciting opportunity to apply for a Visiting Clinical Instructor position in our CAEP-accredited School of Education and Counseling to start August 2027. The successful candidate will teach courses primarily in the Elementary program, primarily the area of literacy. Position Responsibilities & Description The Visiting Clinical Instructor is a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position within the School of Education and Counseling. The successful candidate will teach up to 5 courses each semester, supervise teacher candidates in fields, and support program operations through service and committee work. Key Responsibilities Teach five courses per semester (5-5 load) in literacy and foundational education areas. Attend and participate in Education Program faculty meetings. Engage in service activities that support the School, College, and University, including committee work, as assigned. Collaborate with faculty and staff on program assessment, accreditation, recruitment, and continuous improvement efforts. Perform other duties related to the operation, continued accreditation, and advancement of the educator preparation program. Qualifications/Requirements Master’s or doctoral degree from an accredited program in Education (Literacy related) Minimum of 3 years as a K-12 teacher Licensed, or eligible for a license, as a teacher in the state of Indiana (license does not have to be current) Science of Reading endorsement or similar certificate Preferred Experience: Demonstrated successful teaching courses at university level (literacy methods preferred) Experience with Learning Management Systems for higher education Awareness and knowledge of AI and how to effectively integrate in courses University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. Departments and Schools within the college include: Behavioral Sciences Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health services and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health-related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. School of Education and Counseling Profile The School of Education and Counseling serves over 300 students in undergraduate education programs (elementary and secondary) and graduate program in counseling education.  Degrees from these majors lead to careers as K-12 teachers, special education specialists, and mental health and school counselors. All candidates are prepared to receive licensure following successful completion. Application Information Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references. All inquiries, nominations, and applications will remain confidential; references will not be contacted without notice. Position inquiries may be addressed to Search Chair Dr. Walski at mwalski@pnw.edu . Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/8/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/PNW-Clinical-Assistant-Professor-of-STEM/44325-en_US": {
      "title": "PNW Clinical Assistant Professor of STEM",
      "description": "PNW Clinical Assistant Professor of STEM Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44325 Job Title: PNW Clinical Assistant Professor of STEM City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences at Purdue University Northwest announces an exciting opportunity to apply for a Clinical Assistant Professor position in our CAEP-accredited School of Education and Counseling to start August 2027. The successful candidate will teach courses in the Elementary and Secondary Education programs. Position Responsibilities & Description The Clinical Assistant Professor is a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position within the School of Education and Counseling. The successful candidate will teach up to 5 courses each semester, supervise teacher candidates in fields, and support program operations through service and committee work. Key Responsibilities Teach five courses per semester (5-5 load) in STEM education and related program areas. Supervise teacher candidates during field experiences Attend and participate in Education Program faculty meetings. Engage in service activities that support the School, College, and University, including committee work, as assigned. Collaborate with faculty and staff on program assessment, accreditation, recruitment, and continuous improvement efforts. Perform other duties related to the operation, continued accreditation, and advancement of the educator preparation program. Qualifications/Requirements Required Experience: Master’s or doctoral degree from an accredited program in Education (STEM related) Minimum of 3 years as a K-12 teacher Licensed, or eligible for a license, as a teacher in the state of Indiana (license does not have to be current) Preferred Experience: Supervision of preservice teachers Teaching education courses at university level (science or math methods preferred) Experience with LMS and teaching online courses Awareness and knowledge of AI and how to effectively integrate in courses University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. Departments and Schools within the college include: Behavioral Sciences Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health services and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health-related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. School of Education and Counseling Profile The School of Education and Counseling serves over 300 students in undergraduate education programs (elementary and secondary) and graduate program in counseling education.  Degrees from these majors lead to careers as K-12 teachers, special education specialists, and mental health and school counselors. All candidates are prepared to receive licensure following successful completion. Application Information Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references. In the cover letter, please include your teaching philosophy.  All inquiries, nominations, and applications will remain confidential; references will not be contacted without notice. Position inquiries may be addressed to Search Chair Dr. Patrick Keegan at pjkeegan@pnw.edu Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/8/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Clinical-Assistant-Professor%2C-Practicum-Clinic-Coordinator/44314-en_US": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor, Practicum Clinic Coordinator",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant Professor, Practicum Clinic Coordinator Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44314 Job Title: Clinical Assistant Professor, Practicum Clinic Coordinator City: Hammond Job Description: Job Summary The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences at Purdue University Northwest announces an exciting opportunity to apply for a Clinical Assistant Professor, Practicum Clinic Coordinator position in our CACREP Accredited Master of Science in Education, Clinical Mental Health Counseling and School Counseling Programs to start August 2027. The successful candidate will teach courses in the Counselor Education program and support clinical supervision/instruction within the Community Counseling Clinic at PNW. Position Responsibilities & Description Development/oversight/implementation of CCC policies/procedures in collaboration with Counseling Program Coordinator.  As clinic coordinator, maintain all standards related to relevant accrediting bodies. Assure the provision of counseling services in a manner consistent with accepted professional and ethical standards and state law. Individual and group clinical supervision and instruction of counseling students engaged in CCC clinical activities including: 1) oversee/manage master’s interns in the CCC, including providing clinical supervision, 2) potentially carry a small clinical caseload in the CCC, for teaching and training purposes (e.g., co-therapy; recorded sessions to be used for teaching purposes, etc.) Teach 1- 2 courses per fall, spring, and summer semesters, as needed. Required course instruction for Practicum and Advanced Practicum courses.  Oversee/coordinate clinical master’s practicum sequencing.  Provide didactic training and facilitate new student and adjunct faculty orientations. Participate in program governance (e.g. work collaboratively on accreditation and program improvement efforts) and participate in beneficial service to the school, college, university, community, and profession. Qualifications/Requirements The School of Education and Counseling is seeking applicants who have an earned doctoral degree in counselor education from a CACREP-accredited program and have clinical experience for a minimum of one full academic year. Applicants must be licensed as a clinical mental health in Indiana. Required Experience: Doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited program. At least one year of post degree clinical experience Eligible to be fully licensed as a Mental Health Counselor (LMHC) in Indiana Training in supervision Preferred Experience: Clinical experience with community populations Clinical experience with youth Training and/or certifications in play therapy or expressive arts Experience teaching practicum and/or providing clinical supervision. Experience in administrative role(s) Eligible for School Counselor license in Indiana University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. Departments and Schools within the college include: Behavioral Sciences Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health services and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health-related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. School of Education and Counseling Profile The School of Education and Education programs are accredited by the State of Indiana, and nationally accredited through the Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP). The Counseling Clinical Mental Health and School Counseling programs are accredited through the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP). How to Apply Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, contact information for three references. In the cover letter, please include your teaching philosophy. All inquiries, nominations, and applications will remain confidential; references will not be contacted without notice. Position inquiries may be addressed to Counseling Coordinator and Search Chair Shawn Patrick, Ed.D. –at sjpatric@purdue.edu Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/8/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/LecturerField-Coordinator/44335-en_US": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Field Coordinator",
      "description": "Lecturer/Field Coordinator Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44335 Job Title: Lecturer/Field Coordinator City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary The College of Humanities, Education and Social Sciences at Purdue University Northwest announces an exciting opportunity to apply for a Lecturer/Field Coordinator position in our CAEP accredited School of Education and Counseling and School program to start August 2027. The successful candidate will teach in the Elementary and Secondary education programs and support field placements each semester. Position Responsibilities & Description In addition to teaching one foundations level course and overseeing the student teachers, the Field Coordinator serves as the primary liaison between the School of Education, partner schools, and teacher candidates to ensure high-quality field and clinical experiences. This position coordinates student placements, cultivates and maintains partnerships with school districts, supports teacher candidates throughout their field experiences, and monitors program effectiveness. Key Responsibilities Teaching: Teach one foundations level course (based on experience and expertise) and oversee Student Teaching semester Field Coordinator: Coordinate and secure field and clinical placements for teacher candidates. Develop and maintain partnerships with school districts and community educational organizations. Serve as the primary point of contact for teacher candidates, cooperating teachers, university supervisors, and school administrators. Organize orientations, training, and communications related to field experiences. Monitor candidate progress and address concerns during field placements. Maintain accurate records and documentation to support accreditation, licensure, and program reporting requirements. Support continuous quality improvement efforts to ensure effective and meaningful clinical experiences for teacher candidates. Qualifications/Requirements Masters or Doctorate degree from an accredited program in Education or related field Minimum of 3 years as a K-12 teacher Licensed as a teacher in the state of Indiana (license does not have to be current) Preferred Experience: Supervision of preservice teachers Teaching education courses at university level Field Coordinator Experience (working with principals, placing teacher candidates) Experience in administrative role (s) University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. Departments and Schools within the college include: Behavioral Sciences Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health services and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health-related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. School of Education and Counseling Profile The School of Education and Counseling serves over 300 students in undergraduate education programs (elementary and secondary) and graduate program in counseling education.  Degrees from these majors lead to careers as K-12 teachers, special education specialists, and mental health and school counselors. All candidates are prepared to receive licensure following successful completion. Application Information Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit a cover letter, CV and contact information for three references. In the cover letter, please include your teaching philosophy.  All inquiries, nominations, and applications will remain confidential; references will not be contacted without notice. Position inquiries may be addressed to Search Chair Dr. Grace Pigozzi at pigozzi@pnw.edu Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/6/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Professor-AssistantAssociate-Professor-of-Social-Work/44196-en_US": {
      "title": "Professor Assistant/Associate Professor of Social Work",
      "description": "Professor Assistant/Associate Professor of Social Work Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44196 Job Title: Professor Assistant/Associate Professor of Social Work City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary The Social Work Program in Purdue University Northwest’s Department of Behavioral Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track position, Associate Professor (with tenure granted upon satisfactory review). The position is ideally to begin August 16, 2027.  Candidates must be able to 1) perform high-quality teaching, scholarship and service; and participate in student mentorship and events; 2) demonstrate commitment to promoting anti-oppressive social work practice in their role as faculty member; 3) collaborate with other department programs on curriculum, instruction, and other matters; and 4) teach introductory and advanced practice social work courses across BASW and MSW curriculum; 5) teach 4 courses each semester. While all areas of practice interests will be considered, individuals with focal teaching/research interests in mental health, de-addiction, and trauma-informed care are especially encouraged to apply. This position is benefits eligible. Position Requirements for Associate Professor Earned Doctoral Degree in Social Work or a closely related discipline MSW from a CSWE-accredited program, or recognition of credentials by the International Social Work Degree Recognition and Evaluation Service (ISWDRES) ·         University teaching experience Two years post-MSW practice experience is preferred. University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. In addition to the Behavioral Sciences Department, other administrative units within the college include: Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics, and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College also houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health‐related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. How to Apply To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, contact information for three references, teaching evaluation reports, and up to two publications or writing samples. In the cover letter, please include your teaching philosophy and a statement of your commitment to anti oppressive social work practice with examples of how you have fostered them. Screening of applications will begin immediately. Applications must be received by October 22, 2026 to receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please contact Dr. Shreya Bhandari, Search Committee Chair, at bhanda15@pnw.edu. Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 9/25/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Professor-Assistant/44192-en_US": {
      "title": "Professor Assistant",
      "description": "Professor Assistant Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44192 Job Title: Professor Assistant City: Hammond Job Description: Job Summary The College of Technology at Purdue University Northwest (PNW) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the department of Construction Science and Organizational Leadership. This is a 9-month academic appointment that will commence on January 4, 2027. Exceptional candidates who are not available to begin in January may also be considered for a Fall 2027 start. Qualifications Doctoral degree in engineering or technology, construction engineering, construction management or related field is required by the start of employment. Experience working in the construction engineering/technology field is preferred. Undergraduate degree must be in a relevant area. Candidates with documentation of teaching in a group learning environment and developing industry-relevant laboratory experiments, such as past teaching reviews or awards, are encouraged to apply Responsibilities Teaching graduate and undergraduate courses in various construction management engineering technology and civil engineering technology subjects/fields. Conduct scholarly research leading to refereed journal publications, writing grant proposals. Participate in industrial engagement and various committees. Interacting with regional businesses are encouraged. Research Expectations The Department of Construction Science and Organizational Leadership offers full support when presenting peer review papers at conferences. The department and the university offer several internal opportunities for professional development and for external grant application preparation. Department Profile The Department of Construction Science and Organizational Leadership is housed at the Hammond, Indiana campus and the Construction Management Engineering Technology program is accredited by the Technology Accreditation Commission of  ABET. The department also offers master’s and doctoral programs through the College of Technology Graduate Studies. Benefits Eligibility YES University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW), a premier metropolitan university located in Northwest Indiana, is dedicated to empowering transformational change in its students on its two campuses in Hammond and Westville, Indiana. Part of the Purdue University system and offering more than 60 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, PNW is committed to academic excellence and supports growth. PNW’s colleges, schools, and centers are distinguished through prestigious accreditations, national recognitions, and competitive rankings. PNW’s Hammond campus is located only 30 miles from Chicago with the Westville branch campus just 15 miles from the Indiana Dunes National Park. Fostering a university community full of contrasts – urban and rural, industrial and natural, with a well-established history and forward-thinking mindset – PNW’s two campuses serve its students, many of whom are the first in their families to attend college to make the most of this rich range of resources. Application Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted electronically on the PNW Careers Faculty webpage. A complete application package will include: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Professional References Unofficial Transcript Statement of Teaching Philosophy Research Statement Additional Information Questions may be sent to Search Chair at CSOLSearch@pnw.edu . Visit our website www.pnw.edu for additional information about the university. For information regarding our excellent benefits package, visit www.purdue.edu/benefits . Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 9/24/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Assistant-Professor-of-Social-Work/44195-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Social Work",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Social Work Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 44195 Job Title: Assistant Professor of Social Work City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary Assistant Professor of Social Work – Behavioral Sciences Department Position Description The Social Work Program in Purdue University Northwest’s Department of Behavioral Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track position, as Assistant Professor. The position is ideally to begin August 16, 2027.  Candidates must be able to 1) perform high-quality teaching, scholarship and service; and participate in student mentorship and events; 2) demonstrate commitment to promoting anti-oppressive social work practice in their role as faculty member; 3) collaborate with other department programs on curriculum, instruction, and other matters; and 4) teach introductory and advanced practice social work courses across BASW and MSW curriculum; 5) teach 4 courses each semester (tenure-track assistant professors normally have a reduced teaching load of 3 courses through penultimate year). While all areas of practice interests will be considered, individuals with focal teaching/research interests in mental health, de-addiction, and trauma-informed care are especially encouraged to apply. This position is benefits eligible. Position Requirements for Assistant Professor ·    Earned Doctoral Degree in Social Work or a closely related discipline by August 2027 ·    MSW from a CSWE-accredited program, or recognition of credentials by the International Social Work Degree Recognition and Evaluation Service (ISWDRES) Two years post-MSW practice experience and University teaching experience, preferred University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, IN, and its branch campus in Westville, IN. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. In addition to the Behavioral Sciences Department, other administrative units within the college include: Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics, and Economics Psychology School of Education & Counseling The College also houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health‐related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. How to Apply To apply, submit a cover letter, CV, contact information for three references, teaching evaluation reports, and up to two publications or writing samples. In the cover letter, please include your teaching philosophy and a statement of your commitment to anti oppressive social work practice with examples of how you have fostered them. Screening of applications will begin immediately. Applications must be received by October 22, 2026 to receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please contact Dr. Shreya Bhandari, Search Committee Chair, at bhanda15@pnw.edu. Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 9/24/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Lecturer-of-Sociology/43950-en_US": {
      "title": "Lecturer of Sociology",
      "description": "Lecturer of Sociology Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 43950 Job Title: Lecturer of Sociology City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary Purdue University Northwest’s Department of Behavioral Sciences invites applications for a Lecturer of Sociology to begin August 2027. This position requires a PhD in Sociology by the start date. ABDs will be considered. Candidates must be able to perform high-quality teaching of introduction to sociology and social problems as well as be willing to perform service and participate in professional development activities. College teaching experience is required. Area of primary teaching specialization is open, but the department has needs in research methods, statistics, race and ethnicity, and social inequality. The standard teaching load is the equivalent of five courses (15 credits) per semester. University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement. Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 students, including 6,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master’s and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, Ind., and its branch campus in Westville, Ind., plus additional sites in Northwest Indiana including the Gabis Arboretum near Valparaiso, Ind. PNW’s values reflect the university’s commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report’s annual Best Colleges report, and is recognized as a First Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University. With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. How to Apply To apply, submit a CV, a summary of teaching evaluations from the past 4 semesters, a statement of teaching philosophy, and a list of at least 3 professional references. Screening of applications will begin immediately. Applications must be received by October 30, 2026, to receive full consideration. For questions about this position, please contact Dr. David Nalbone, Behavioral Sciences Department Chair, at dnalbone@purdue.edu. Employment is contingent upon successful completion of background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 9/24/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/PNW-Lecturer-of-Nursing/43209-en_US": {
      "title": "PNW Lecturer of Nursing",
      "description": "PNW Lecturer of Nursing Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 43209 Job Title: PNW Lecturer of Nursing City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary Join an innovative, high performing team of faculty and staff who are building the future of nursing.  The College of Nursing is focused on excellence through innovation, applying the best available evidence throughout the curriculum as well as in our teaching approach, and engaging the community for greater impact.  The College of Nursing is a National League for Nursing Center of Excellence and is home to Indiana Center for Evidence Based Nursing Practice, a JBI Affiliate Center. Purdue University Northwest's College of Nursing invites applications for the full time academic position of Lecturer of Nursing for an academic year appointment starting August 17, 2026. Education Earned master's degree in nursing Earned doctorate in nursing (DNP) or (PhD) preferred Responsibilities Use evidence-based approaches to teaching in classroom and healthcare settings for the undergraduate program. Contribute to curriculum development and revision. Participate in university, professional, and community service. Engage in clinical practice. Rank and salary are commensurate with experience and education. Experience Exceptional, experienced based subject matter, expertise in Medical Surgical Nursing. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated teaching or precepting experience. Commitment to life-long learning and eagerness to serve students. Candidates with clinical, classroom teaching, simulation and online teaching experience are strongly encouraged to apply. University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW) is a student-centered university that transforms lives through innovative education, impactful research and community engagement.  Located in Northwest Indiana in the greater Chicago area and near the Indiana Dunes National Park, PNW serves approximately 9,000 core students, in a wide range of academic programs at the baccalaureate, master's and applied doctoral levels. Purdue Northwest employs more than 1,000 faculty and staff members for operations at its main campus in Hammond, Indiana, and its branch campus in Westville, Indiana, plus additional sites in Northwest Indiana including the Gabis Arboretum near Valparaiso, Indiana.  PNW\"s values reflect the university's commitment to respect, innovation, a student-centric university, and excellence. PNW is consistently ranked among the best public regional universities in U.S. News & World Report's annual Best Colleges report and is recognized as a First  Scholars institution as well an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University.  With its vision to cultivate leaders, inspire excellence, and impact our world, PNW advances the socioeconomic mobility of its students and positively impacts regional development. College Profile The College of Nursing offers undergraduate degrees including a traditional BSN, second degree accelerated BSN, and an online RN to BSN.  We offer several MSN options including two advanced practice roles (family nurse practitioner and adult-gerontology clinical nurse specialist) and two advanced online specialties (nurse educator and nurse executive).  Our post-master's DNP program has a focus on translation science. How to Apply Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until position is filled.  Start date:  August 17, 2026. To apply, applicants should submit the following: Letter of application Curriculum vita includes references Statement of teaching philosophy PNW offers an attractive salary and an excellent benefits package.  For information regarding the benefits package, visit: www.purdue.edu/benefits All inquiries, nominations, and applications will remain confidential; references will not be contacted without notice. Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 7/31/26 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Assistant-Professor-of-Psychology/39839-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 39839 Job Title: Assistant Professor of Psychology City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary Join our dynamic team of faculty and staff at an exciting time of innovation and growth! The Department of Psychology at Purdue University Northwest invites applications for an Assistant Professor of Psychology, tenure-track faculty appointment, to begin Fall 2027. The successful applicant will serve as a core faculty member in our well-established and highly popular undergraduate psychology program, as well as our new psychology graduate programs.  We’re looking for a strong teacher, scholar, and clinician interested in shaping these cutting-edge graduate training programs with particular emphasis on integrating social justice, advocacy, contemporary best practices, and cultural competence into clinical psychology. Area of scholarship is open, though we are particularly interested in the following specialty areas: psychological assessment, practicum instruction and supervision, psychotherapy, multicultural psychology, trauma, child/adolescent psychology, forensic psychology, and applied research methods (e.g., program evaluation, qualitative research). The position is a full-time 9- month appointment and  responsibilities include some or all of the following: teaching graduate and undergraduate courses, developing an active program of research/scholarship, research mentoring for undergraduate and graduate students (including supervising Psy.D. dissertation projects), clinical supervision for in-house psychological assessment training clinic, and contributing to service activities in the department, college, and university (including APA accreditation process). The typical teaching load is 3 classes/semester (and may be distributed across both campuses); however, teaching and workload distributions are negotiable, depending on each faculty member’s specific goals and strengths. The position is open to clinical or counseling psychologists who completed an APA-accredited predoctoral internship, graduated from an APA-accredited doctoral program, and are eligible for licensure and endorsement as a Health Service Provider in Psychology (HSPP) in Indiana or will be eligible within two years (clinical release time and in-house supervision can be negotiated). Required Qualifications Successful completion of an APA-accredited predoctoral internship Completion of an APA-accredited doctoral program in clinical or counseling psychology Licensed or license eligible as a Health Service Provider in Psychology (HSPP) in Indiana within two years of appointment (including completion of postdoctoral hours) Demonstrated teaching ability Graduate and undergraduate teaching experience Evidence of the beginning of an independent program of research/scholarship University Profile Purdue University Northwest (PNW), a premier metropolitan university located in Northwest Indiana, is dedicated to empowering transformational change in its students on its two campuses in Hammond and Westville, Indiana. Part of the Purdue University system and offering more than 60 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, PNW is committed to academic excellence, supports growth, and celebrates diversity. PNW’s colleges, schools and centers are distinguished through prestigious accreditations, national recognitions, and competitive rankings. PNW’s Hammond campus is located only 30 miles from Chicago with the Westville branch campus just 15 miles from the Indiana Dunes National Park. Fostering a university community full of contrasts—urban and rural, industrial and natural, with a well-established history and forward-thinking mindset—PNW’s two campuses serve its students, many of whom are the first in their families to attend college to make the most of this rich range of resources. Moreover, PNW has been designated as a Hispanic-serving public institution dedicated to excellence in teaching and learning and committed to the advancement of a diverse and under-served student population. College Profile The College of Humanities, Education, and Social Sciences serves over 1,400 undergraduate students and 170 graduate students across a wide range of programs. In addition to the Psychology Department, other administrative units within the college include: Behavioral Sciences Communication and Creative Arts English and World Languages History, Philosophy, Politics, and Economics School of Education & Counseling The College also houses the Community Counseling Center that provides quality affordable mental health and the Institute for Social and Policy Research, which uses social science and policy analysis methods to find solutions to educational, social, and health‐related challenges facing the citizens of Northwest Indiana and the Chicago metropolitan region. FLSA Status Exempt How to Apply Review of applications will begin on January 5, 2027, and will continue until position is filled. Start date: August 2027 To apply, applicants should submit the following: Cover letter that describes your clinical, teaching, and research experience and interests. Be sure to address your specific interest in and rationale for joining a department with a more practitioner-scholar academic training model (as compared to a more research-intensive or scientist-practitioner model) Curriculum vitae Evaluations of teaching (if available) Contact information for three professional references Finalist will be required to provide transcripts More information about the department and its programs can be found at www.pnw.edu/psychology. For inquiries, please contact search committee chair, Dr. Gisele M. Casanova at gmcasano@pnw.edu . Employment is contingent upon completion of successful background check. Purdue University Northwest is an equal opportunity/equal access university. Apply now Posting Start Date: 12/1/25 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/Limited-Term-Lecturer%2C-Behavioral-Sciences/39543-en_US": {
      "title": "Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences",
      "description": "Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 39543 Job Title: Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences City: Hammond Job Description: Job Summary The Department of Behavioral Sciences is seeking applications for Limited Term Lecturers (LTLs) in the social work program for the fall 2026 and spring 2027 semesters. LTL positions are temporary, non-benefits eligible, part-time positions hired each semester on an as-needed basis. The Primary Responsibilities of successful candidates will be to: 1. Teach on campus and/or online courses in Bachelor of Arts in Social Work (BASW) or Master of Social Work (MSW) program. 2. Follow the syllabus and curricula provided according to the most recent Educational and Professional Accreditation Standards (EPAS) set by Council of Social Work Education (CSWE). 3. Maintain office hours and provide feedback to students in a timely manner. For practicum courses, collect and submit the practicum data from the Practicum Supervisors to the Program Director for assessment purposes. 4. Maintain FERPA and Title X certification. 5. Understand and adhere to University policies and guidelines. 6. Have competency in effective and appropriate in-person and online (synchronous and asynchronous) delivery instruction. Education Master’s degree in Social Work from a CSWE-accredited program. PhD or DSW in Social Work or allied field is preferable but not required. Experience Minimum 2 years of post -MSW practice experience preferably in substance abuse/deaddiction Core Competencies Strong interpersonal, presentation, and written and verbal communication skills and willingness and ability to work in a collaborative setting Demonstrated experience teaching at the college level or relevant professional experience Department Profile The Behavioral Sciences department is one of the most intellectually diverse at PNW, and is comprised of several undergraduate (behavioral sciences, criminal justice, sociology, social work) and graduate programs (couple and family therapy, social work) with over 300 undergraduate majors. How to Apply Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, applicants should submit the following: 1) a cover letter specifically addressing required experience; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) list of three professional references. A background check will be required for employment in this position. Employment would begin on August 17, 2026, and continue on a semester basis. Additional Information Purdue University Northwest (PNW), a premier metropolitan university located in Northwest Indiana, is dedicated to empowering transformational change in its students on its two campuses in Hammond and Westville, Indiana. Part of the Purdue University system and offering more than 70 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, PNW is committed to academic excellence, supports growth, and celebrates diversity. PNW’s colleges, schools and centers are distinguished through prestigious accreditations, national recognitions, and competitive rankings. PNW’s Hammond campus is located only 30 miles from Chicago with the Westville branch campus just 15 miles from Indiana Dunes National Park. Fostering a university community full of contrasts—urban and rural, industrial and natural, with a well-established history and forward-thinking mindset—PNW’s two campuses serve its students, many of whom are the first in their families to attend college to make the most of this rich range of resources. Moreover, PNW has been designated as a Hispanic-serving public institution dedicated to excellence in teaching and learning and committed to the advancement of a diverse and under-served student population. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/31/25 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
    },
    "https://careers.purdue.edu/PNW/job/W-Limited-Term-Lecturer%2C-Behavioral-Sciences/37875-en_US": {
      "title": "W Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences",
      "description": "W Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences Job Details | Purdue University By continuing to use and navigate this website, you are agreeing to the use of cookies. Accept Close Skip to main content Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Search Jobs Home Page Internal Applicants View Jobs Faculty Staff Student Language English (United States) View Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Req Id: 37875 Job Title: W Limited Term Lecturer, Behavioral Sciences City: Hammond and Westville Job Description: Job Summary The Department of Behavioral Sciences is seeking applications for Limited Term Lecturers (LTLs) in Behavioral Sciences. LTL positions are temporary, non-benefits eligible, part-time positions hired each semester on an as-needed basis. The Primary Responsibilities of successful candidates will be to: Teach on campus and/or online courses in sociology or statistics. Follow the syllabus and curricula provided. Maintain office hours and provide feedback to students in a timely manner. Maintain FERPA and Title X certification. Assist with program-related duties and administrative tasks, including placement, orientation, proctoring, student evaluations, curriculum and program review. Understand and adhere to University policies and guidelines. Have competency in effective and appropriate in-person and online (synchronous and asynchronous) delivery instruction. Education, Experience, Training and/or Certifications Master’s degree in Sociology or a related field (minimum 18 hours of graduate work in Sociology) Strong interpersonal, presentation, and written and verbal communication skills Demonstrated experience teaching at the college level or relevant professional experience Demonstrated ability to communicate both orally and in writing in English Purdue University Northwest (PNW) Purdue University Northwest (PNW), a premier metropolitan university located in Northwest Indiana, is dedicated to empowering transformational change in its students on its two campuses in Hammond and Westville, Indiana. Part of the Purdue University system and offering more than 70 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, PNW is committed to academic excellence, supports growth, and celebrates diversity. PNW’s colleges, schools and centers are distinguished through prestigious accreditations, national recognitions, and competitive rankings. PNW’s Hammond campus is located only 30 miles from Chicago with the Westville branch campus just 15 miles from the Indiana Dunes National Park. Fostering a university community full of contrasts—urban and rural, industrial and natural, with a well-established history and forward-thinking mindset—PNW’s two campuses serve its students, many of whom are the first in their families to attend college to make the most of this rich range of resources. Moreover, PNW has been designated as a Hispanic-serving public institution dedicated to excellence in teaching and learning and committed to the advancement of a diverse and under-served student population. Department Profile The Behavioral Sciences department is one of the most intellectually diverse at PNW, and is comprised of several undergraduate (behavioral sciences, criminal justice, sociology, social work) and graduate programs (couple and family theray, social work) with over 300 undergraduate majors. How to Apply Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, applicants should submit the following: 1) a cover letter specifically addressing required experience; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) list of three professional references. A background check will be required for employment in this position. Employment would begin on August 18, 2025, and continue on a semester basis. Purdue University is an equal opportunity/equal access university. FLSA Status Exempt Apply now Posting Start Date: 10/31/25 Search Jobs View All Jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. © 2018 Purdue University Northwest | An equal access/equal opportunity university"
